From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Petar Tijanic <Petar_Tijanic@comcast.com>
Date: Fri, 12 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] gamecontrollerdb: carry the RDK controller overrides

The 0.2 recipe shipped a hand-picked set of controller mappings on top of the
SDL database. 1.0 bundles its own SDL snapshot as a GResource, so those were
lost. Some matter: a few controllers are clones that share a GUID with another
device, so the stock entry maps them wrong (the RedStorm gamer Choice GUID is
the Joy-Con (L) upstream, the GameSir T4 Pro GUID is the Ipega PG 9069). The
rest keep the layout the boxes were validated against. The loader reads the
file top to bottom and replaces on duplicate GUID, so appending makes them win.

x: and y: follow the upstream SDL convention here: x: is the left face button,
y: is the top face button, matching every other entry in this database.

Do not swap them back. The 0.2 entries these were taken from carried x: and y:
transposed, to cancel out a bug on the consumer side: wpe-backend-rdk reads the
evdev code libmanette hands it as an Xbox label, but BTN_X is #defined as
BTN_NORTH (the top button) and BTN_Y as BTN_WEST (the left button), so it
resolved every mapping to the opposite face button. Two errors that cancelled.
The libmanette 1.0 consumers (ManetteGamepadProvider in WPE WebKit, and
toStandardGamepadButton_v1 in wpe-backend-rdk) use the ManetteButton enum
directly and do not have that bug, so the compensation has to be absent here.
The Amazon Luna reports its select and home keys as keyboard codes (KEY_BACK and
KEY_HOMEPAGE) rather than gamepad button codes. 0.2 and 1.0 give those two keys
their hardware index in opposite order - the RDK 0.2 patch assigns Mode before
Select with two hard-coded ifs, while 1.0 walks the key codes in ascending order
and so reaches KEY_BACK (158) before KEY_HOMEPAGE (172). back: and guide: must
therefore differ between the two databases; do not copy them across.

